# AI provider abstraction

Geos uses AI for correlation labeling, summaries, translation, classification, entity extraction, and embeddings. All of it goes through one abstraction so the product is provider-agnostic, air-gap capable, and cost-controlled.

## Core rules

- **All AI calls go through the `AiProvider` trait** in `crates/core`. Never call a vendor SDK/HTTP endpoint directly from feature code. Supported backends: Anthropic, OpenAI, and local Ollama, selected via config.
- **No hardcoded providers, models, endpoints, or API keys.** Read them from config/env (validated at startup). Secrets never appear in code, logs, or the repo (`git-commits.mdc`, `api-error-shape.mdc`).
- **Versioned prompts.** Prompts and their version live in `crates/core`; changing a prompt is a deliberate, reviewed change (note it in the ADR/commit). This keeps outputs reproducible and debuggable.

## Air-gapped / skippable

- **Every AI step must be skippable** (or routed to local Ollama) so core features run with no outbound internet. Always provide a deterministic, non-AI fallback path (e.g. ingestion still stores events without AI summaries; correlation still runs the geo/temporal/vector stage without LLM labeling).
- Embeddings must be generatable locally (Ollama) for air-gapped deployments; the embedding dimension must match the pgvector index (`geospatial-postgis.mdc`).

## Cost & abuse control

- **Enforce per-tenant token/cost budgets.** Meter usage into `quota_usage`; refuse or degrade gracefully when a tenant exceeds its plan's AI quota rather than running uncapped spend.
- Use **caching** (don't re-embed/re-summarize unchanged content) and **batching** for candidate clusters.

## Privacy

- **Never send PII or restricted content to a cloud provider when the tenant/policy forbids it** (`privacy-gdpr.mdc`). Respect per-tenant data-handling settings; prefer local models when content is sensitive. Scrub/avoid secrets in prompts.

## Before "done"

- Confirm the new AI usage routes through `AiProvider`, has a non-AI/offline fallback, respects budgets, and does not leak PII to disallowed providers.
